N+1 Problem
===========
 POST /graphql
query {
  authors {
    id
    name
    posts {
      title
    }
  }
}


On the server side, a naive implementation would execute queries in this sequence:

   1. One (1) initial query to fetch all authors: SELECT * FROM authors;
   2.  The server then loops through the list of authors and, for each one, triggers N additional
        queries to fetch their posts:
        SELECT * FROM posts WHERE author_id = 1;
        SELECT * FROM posts WHERE author_id = 2;
        ...and so on for N authors.

If there are 50 authors, this single GraphQL query results in 51 database round trips.
This high number of requests increases latency, wastes server resources,
and can lead to timeouts and poor user experience as data sets grow.


Even if several posts share the same author, the server will still issue
duplicate queries unless you implement deduplication or batching manually.

The Solution: DataLoaders
--------------------------

The most common and recommended solution to the N+1 problem is using a utility
called DataLoader (or similar implementations in other languages/frameworks).
DataLoader works by batching and caching requests within a single GraphQL query execution:

    Batching:
        It collects all the individual requests for a specific type of data that occur within a
        short time frame (e.g., all author IDs needed for all posts). It then executes a single, optimized
        database query (e.g., using a WHERE IN (...) clause) to fetch all the required related data at once.
        This reduces the number of database calls from N to 1 for the related data.

    Caching:
        It stores the results of a query in a per-request cache, ensuring that
        if the same data is requested again during the same operation, it is returned
        from the cache rather than triggering another database call.

        {
          me { name }
          currentProject {
            owner { name } # This is 'me' again!
          }
        }


By implementing DataLoader, the 51 queries in the previous example can be reduced to just two: one for the
   authors and one batched query for all the posts, drastically improving performance and
   scalability.



